.TH "vsearch-sff_convert" "1" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --sff_convert \[em] convert an SFF file to fastq
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--sff_convert\f[R] \f[I]sfffile\f[R]
\f[B]--fastqout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--sff_convert\f[R] converts the reads stored in
an SFF (Standard Flowgram Format) file to fastq.
SFF is a binary format used by Roche 454 and early Ion Torrent PGM
sequencing platforms (see \f[V]vsearch-sff(5)\f[R]).
.PP
The output fastq file is written to \f[V]--fastqout\f[R].
The quality encoding offset can be set with \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R]
(default 33, phred+33).
Output quality scores are always clamped to the range set by
\f[V]--fastq_qminout\f[R] and \f[V]--fastq_qmaxout\f[R], whose defaults
are 0 and 41: SFF quality values above 41 are silently reduced to 41
unless \f[V]--fastq_qmaxout\f[R] is raised.
.PP
While converting, vsearch reports the number of reads, the flows per
read, the key sequence, the index type (when an index is present) and
the minimal, average and maximal read lengths \[em] to standard error,
or to the log file when \f[V]--log\f[R] is used.
A warning is emitted when the SFF index is missing, when the index is at
an unusual position in the file, or when additional data follow the end
of the SFF content.
.PP
Each SFF read stores clipping coordinates that indicate low-quality or
adapter regions at the ends of the sequence.
By default, no clipping is applied: the full sequence is written, with
bases that would be clipped converted to lower case and the rest in
upper case.
Use \f[V]--sff_clip\f[R] to apply the clipping and write only the
retained region in upper case.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS mandatory options
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write converted sequences to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fastq format (see
\f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
.SS core options
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R] 33|64
Specify the \f[I]offset\f[R] used as the basis for the fastq quality
score when writing fastq output files.
For example, an offset of 33 means that a quality value of 41 is
represented by the 74th ASCII symbol (33 + 41 = 74), which is `J'.
See \f[V]ascii(7)\f[R] for a view of the ASCII character set.
The offset value is either 33 or 64, default is 33.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmaxout\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the maximum quality score used when writing fastq files.
The default is 41, which is usual for recent Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files.
Older formats may use a maximum quality score of 40.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qminout\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the minimum quality score used when writing fastq files.
The offset (see \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R]) plus the minimum score must
be at least 33, so the value may be negative when the output offset is
64.
The default is 0, which is usual for recent Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files.
Older formats may use scores between -5 and 2.
.TP
\f[V]--sff_clip\f[R]
Clip sequences at both ends as indicated by the clipping coordinates
stored in the SFF file (\f[V]clip_qual_left\f[R],
\f[V]clip_qual_right\f[R], \f[V]clip_adapter_left\f[R],
\f[V]clip_adapter_right\f[R]).
Without this option, no clipping is performed and bases that would have
been clipped are written in lower case, while the remaining bases are in
upper case.
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--label_suffix\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the suffix \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers when writing fasta
or fastq files.
For example, with \f[V]--label_suffix \[dq];status=healthy\[dq]\f[R],
sequence header `>seq1' becomes `>seq1;status=healthy'.
.TP
\f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
Add a sequence length annotation (\f[V];length=integer\f[R]) to each
sequence header when writing fasta or fastq files.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Replace sequence headers with the prefix \f[I]string\f[R] and a ticker
(1, 2, 3, etc.).
For example, with \f[V]--relabel \[dq]cluster:\[dq]\f[R], the first
sequence header becomes `>cluster:1', the second sequence header becomes
`>cluster:2', and so on.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R]
Retain old sequence identifiers by including them at the end of the new
headers, after a space.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the MD5 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The MD5 digest is a 128-bit value (16 bytes), represented using a string
of 32 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]md5(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_self\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the sequence itself.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the SHA1 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The SHA1 digest is a 160-bit value (20 bytes), represented using a
string of 40 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]sha1(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--sample\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the given sample identifier \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers
when writing fasta or fastq files.
For instance, if \f[I]string\f[R] is `ABC', the text
\f[V];sample=ABC\f[R] will be added to the headers.
\f[I]string\f[R] is silently truncated at the first `;' or whitespace
character (space, tab, newline, carriage return, vertical tab or form
feed), so such characters should not be used in \f[I]string\f[R].
Other characters (alphabetical, numerical and punctuations) are
accepted.
.TP
\f[V]--sizeout\f[R]
Add abundance annotations to sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Add the pattern \f[V];size=integer\f[R].
If option \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is not used, abundance values are set to 1
for all entries.
If \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is used, existing abundance annotations are simply
reported to output files.
.SS ignored options
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R]
Command is not multithreaded, option has no effect (a warning is printed
when more than one thread is requested).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Convert an SFF file to fastq using the default phred+33 encoding:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --sff_convert reads.sff \[rs]
    --fastqout reads.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Convert and apply clipping coordinates from the SFF file:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --sff_convert reads.sff \[rs]
    --sff_clip \[rs]
    --fastqout reads_clipped.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Convert with phred+64 encoding and restrict quality scores to 0\[en]40:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --sff_convert reads.sff \[rs]
    --fastq_asciiout 64 \[rs]
    --fastq_qmaxout 40 \[rs]
    --fastqout reads.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
